## 3. Product truth, intended use, and non-negotiable safety boundary

### 3.1 What AirTon is intended to become

AirTon is a proposed portable, affordable, non-contact home eye-pressure monitoring system. The first product direction confirmed in the repository is **home IOP monitoring for patients already under a doctor's care**. Patients follow an individualized monitoring plan established by their treating clinician. The device is expected to guide positioning, check readiness, reject unsuitable acquisitions, identify the eye, retain measurement quality and provenance, and make a traceable history available to the assigned care team.

The concept combines a proposed air-puff stimulus, optical sensing, signal processing / future calibrated pressure estimation, a device-side interface, and connected reporting. The mobile app is the patient/caregiver companion and a mobile care-team review surface for the prototype phase.

### 3.2 What is not verified today

The repository explicitly says:

- There is no verified AirTon physical pressure-estimation model or clinically qualified model.
- The recovered Keras and PyTorch work concerns retinal-image classification research, not IOP estimation. Its provenance and clinical qualification are unresolved.
- The available simulator creates fictional values and synthetic raw-sample payloads. It does not model a physical pressure waveform or a real acquisition.
- The recovered React web interface has simulated role selection, mock readings, mock patients, and illustrative business data. It has no real authentication, device link, production backend, or clinical service.
- The local Python API is synthetic-only and loopback-bound. It is not a production or mobile-facing clinical API.
- No approved acquisition safety limits, physical interlocks, BLE protocol, production cloud account, care-team service, or clinical release authorization are established by the repository.
- Monitoring schedules, repetitions, retry limits, clinical alert thresholds, calibration intervals, pneumatic limits, and optical exposure limits are deliberately `null` in `config/home-monitoring.json`.

Treat these as hard constraints, not optional disclaimers.

### 3.3 Forbidden app behavior

The app must never:

1. Claim to diagnose, screen for, rule out, or confirm glaucoma, or infer disease status from IOP alone.
2. Display an invented “low / medium / high glaucoma risk” label, an unvalidated normal/abnormal pressure badge, an automated red/green clinical threshold, or a recommendation to change medication or treatment.
3. Suggest medication changes, dosage changes, stopping treatment, self-triage, or emergency disposition.
4. Treat a synthetic measurement, retinal classifier result, random number, or demo calibration ID as a real clinical result.
5. Convert a rejected acquisition into a number. A rejected acquisition must have `iop_estimate_mmhg: null` and one or more explicit quality-rejection reasons.
6. Represent a locally saved item as delivered, a delivered item as read, or a read item as clinically reviewed. These are distinct states.
7. Claim that a doctor is continuously monitoring the app, that notifications are urgent clinical alerts, or that a clinician will respond within a promised time.
8. Trigger a physical stimulus, air puff, pump, valve, motor, laser, or other actuator from a phone command. No hardware actuation is part of this task.
9. Connect to an assumed or guessed BLE UUID, device protocol, sensor, or hardware endpoint. The device communication contract has not been established.
10. Upload actual patient information or health data to a mock endpoint, public service, analytics system, or GitHub repository.

Every demo screen containing measurements, records, patient profiles, device state, or reports must have clear persistent demo labeling. Use text and visual treatment together; do not rely on a tiny footnote or color alone.

## 4. Product personas and role-based experience

Create a role-based demo experience with three spaces. Role selection is a **demo-only persona switch**, not authentication or authorization.

### A. Patient / trained caregiver

Primary experience: very simple, reassuring, accessible, and low cognitive load. The user should understand what to do next without needing medical or technical knowledge.

Core needs:

- See whether the app is in simulation mode and whether a device is actually connected (in this version it is not).
- View the care plan only when a clinician-defined plan is present in the fixture. Do not invent a schedule or threshold when the source configuration is `null`.
- See a simple session-status card and the most recent synthetic session, if any.
- Start a guided **simulated** session that explains the steps but does not operate physical hardware.
- Confirm right eye (OD) or left eye (OS) in the workflow and retain separate eye records.
- Understand accepted versus rejected acquisition and the reason for rejection.
- Review a chronological measurement history and detail/provenance screen.
- See local-save / pending-sync / delivered / clinician-review states separately.
- View the assigned care-team contact details included in mock data, and see clinician-authored instructions as read-only content.
- Manage ordinary app settings such as language, text size, notification preferences, and demo reset.

### B. Treating clinician

Primary experience: concise, traceable review of assigned patients and all sessions, including failures and missing data. It is not a diagnostic decision-support engine.

Core needs:

- View a mock assigned-patient roster and search/filter it.
- Open a patient profile and inspect the full chronological history for OD and OS.
- Review accepted and rejected acquisitions, quality reasons, timestamps, provenance, delivery state, and previous amendments.
- Acknowledge that a record has been reviewed, request follow-up/reacquisition, or add a note in the demo workflow.
- Create or edit an illustrative care-plan schedule only through explicit clinician actions. Preserve unresolved values as “Not configured”; do not prefill clinical limits, retry counts, or alert thresholds.
- View a review queue containing delivered-but-not-yet-reviewed sample records.
- Distinguish administrative receipt from a clinician's actual review action.

All clinician actions are simulated and append-only in the local demo store. A note or amendment must show the mock author and timestamp. Do not silently overwrite a previous observation or review event.

### C. Clinic administrator / device-service staff

Primary experience: operational visibility, not clinical interpretation.

Core needs:

- View mock clinic / staff / patient assignment records.
- View a mock device inventory with serial, model, firmware, calibration identifier/status, last service record, and assignment state.
- View enrolment and training status as demonstration data.
- View service and calibration-history examples, with unresolved values clearly marked.
- See high-level workflow counts such as records awaiting sync or clinician review, but never present fabricated revenue, MRR, ROI, or business performance as real.
- Do not allow an admin persona to alter clinical observations or act as a clinician unless an explicit clinician role is separately selected.

For a compact mobile release, patient and clinician flows are the priority. Admin/service views may be a smaller but functional section. Keep the architecture open for a separate web-based administrative console later.

## 5. Recommended technical direction

Use **React Native + Expo + TypeScript**, Android-first and structured so iOS can be added without a rewrite. Use the latest mutually compatible stable Expo SDK / React Native versions available at implementation time; verify package compatibility from official documentation, pin installed versions in the lockfile, and avoid blindly using “latest” for every transitive dependency.

Use an Expo **development build / native project workflow**, not Expo Go as the production target. The future device integration may require native BLE libraries or other native modules that are not bundled in Expo Go. Expo documentation explains that development builds support custom native modules and production-quality projects; see:

- `https://docs.expo.dev/develop/development-builds/faq/`
- `https://docs.expo.dev/workflow/using-libraries/`
- `https://docs.expo.dev/build-reference/android-builds/`

Preferred building blocks (use only where they add value):

- Expo Router for typed, maintainable navigation.
- TypeScript strict mode.
- Zustand or a similarly small state store for demo/session/UI state.
- TanStack Query only where asynchronous repository patterns make it useful; do not add needless complexity for a deterministic local demo.
- `expo-sqlite` for local demonstration records and append-only review events; keep the storage adapter replaceable.
- `expo-secure-store` for future device/session credentials only when real credential flows are implemented. Do not invent credentials for the demo.
- A maintained React Native chart package compatible with the chosen Expo SDK for eye-specific trend visualization.
- A maintained icon set with consistent stroke weights.
- Expo Notifications only for clearly labeled, local demo reminders; never represent them as a clinical alert service.
- React Native Testing Library and Jest/Vitest-compatible tooling for tests, selected according to Expo support at implementation time.

If a different technical choice is demonstrably better, explain why in `apps/mobile/README.md` before adopting it. Do not add a backend, cloud provider, paid service, Firebase project, EAS account dependency, or external analytics provider just to make the prototype appear complete.

